								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=522.msg8135#msg8135">Re: the streets code..</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #24 on:</b> December 12, 2006, 05:16:15 pm &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=522.msg8128#msg8128">Quote from: Cin7 on December 12, 2006, 05:01:51 pm</a></div><div class="quote">no matter how intelligent, motivated and convicted immortal technique may be, lets set something straight here. personally, i think its unfair to even try and make the connection between what an MC is saying on wax and revolutionaries. REAL revolutionaries are killed, crippled and imprisoned everday around the world fighting LITERALLY, not figuratively or creatively, for what the believe in. thats why I have nothing but the utmost respect for the peopl ei fought overseas, because they are the manifestation in the EXTREME or a self motivated person standing the hard stance for what they believe in. Th Black Panther Party for self defense is the last thing comparable to a revolutionary group america has seen, and the countless militia gfroups in iraq and afghansitan are examples of what a real revolutionary stands for. even though i dont agree with them, i respect them.<br /><br />do you think Immortal Technique would be willing to die, lose an arm or leg or be permanently crippled or spend the rest of his life in prison due to his convictions about independant vs. major label music? no. not even &quot; i doubt it&quot;, hell no he wouldnt.<br />i totally dig the point your making, and IM NOT coming at you on the argumentative level, i just think that  a lot of cats toss the word &quot;revolutionary&quot; around and to them its an abstraction or an empty metaphor repeated over and over again in popular media and college classes and music, when the reality is that the true revolutionary is to be feared, honored, respected, and taken very, very seriously.<br /></div><br />I disagree with you here. I agree with you on what a revolutionary <i>is</i>, but disagree with you on what a revolutionary<i> is not</i>.<br /><br />The United States is completely different than Iraq. The United States is completely different than most countries that have people who fit your difinition of a revolutionary. I believe that there are people in the United States who are ready to kill and die for their beliefs, but realize that that is not an effective from of struggle in our society. The conditions are different in the US than from most countries that have active revolutionary armies, and because of the different conditions, different strategies are needed. At this point in history in the US, a teacher or artist who changes heart and minds with strategic words is more effective at bringing the revolution than an armed millitia fighting armed battles against their enemy (whether it be the government, corporations, etc) who would get slaughtered by military and law enforcement. If armed struggle is strategic for changing the world, then it&#039;s revolutionary. But just because you hold a gun and are ready to die for your convitions doesn&#039;t mean you&#039;re a revolutionary. It&#039;s all about context and strategy. What is going to be most effective at achieving your short term and long term goals with an understanding of the concrete conditions before you.<br /><br />Ok, now I&#039;m the one rambling...</div>
