								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=522.msg8145#msg8145"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=522.msg8145#msg8145">Re: the streets code..</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #28 on:</b> December 12, 2006, 06:36:15 pm &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">sorry i should&#039;ve explained his sentiments toward independent labels. it&#039;s something to the effect of &quot;independent labels not that i do not trust you, because YOU&#039;RE ONLY INDEPENDENT UNTIL YOU&#039;RE COMMERCIAL&quot; it&#039;s something like that i can&#039;t remember the exact line. i wasn&#039;t saying i.t. was a revolutionary &#039;cause he really hasn&#039;t made good on any of the claims he&#039;s made in his music. i was just saying we have to make sure we don&#039;t turn into that which we fight. some of the iraqi revolutionaries are a pretty good example of groups abandoning their principles in the pursuit of victory. i just didn&#039;t make it clear enough. in retrospect there&#039;s no way i could expect you to be able to be able to come up with that on the spot. it&#039;s a lot easier to understand vague language when you write it than when you read it.<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">there is no black and white in LAW</div><br />mostly correct. people should analyze cases individually, for sure. but there are some laws that are always unfair.<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">that some people are gonna get fucked over and a lotta peole are gonna get thier personal moralities and feelings stepped on.</div><br />the government should protect minorities, methinks, though psychopaths shouldn&#039;t qualify here. if the government is stepping on a lot of feet with a law whose relaxation or elimination wouldn&#039;t redeploy that pedal pressure to another group, then they should make that relaxation or get rid of the law. allowing people to sell and smoke weed (in privacy in the latter case), for example, wouldn&#039;t have a dramatically adverse effect on those opposed to its legalization. government is a necessary evil, until a later time. its chief concern should be protecting the people from each other, and the reduction of freedoms should take place only where that purpose is given substantial aid. taxing the wealthy to provide funding for welfare programs (though not the current check in the mail welfare system) is, en mi opinion, a good idea until such time as popular democracy is practical. but if you want to legalize marijuana or gay marriage, you&#039;re allowing more freedom without encroaching on other people. if you&#039;re not gay and don&#039;t condone homosexuality, you probably won&#039;t get married to another person of the same sex, so the absence of restrictions will have minimal effect on you. if the government isn&#039;t doing it&#039;s job of ceding powers to the people, the people must seize these powers either by overt or covert action. feigning ignorance in a court case would be an example of the latter. if a dude sells an ounce of chron fair and square, makes sure his customer is fully aware of the health effects of smoking, and disposes of the plastic bag in the appropriate waste facility, i see no reason to sit on the stand and tell anyone he did it. i bet the guy who bought it isn&#039;t against marijuana, either, so any harm it does to him is harm that he expects and accepts. he is making an informed, minor decision about his own body, and i as a potential witness against his supplier think that i should have no bearing on that decision. that&#039;s just me, the purpose of this thread was to hear from you<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">i think heads need to realize that the only way &quot;the system&quot; works is by being unwavering</div><br />yep. so heads unaffiliated with &quot;the system&quot;, who believe that it&#039;s a temporary necessity, have the power to work around its imperfections by withholding/falsifying testimony. i&#039;m egotistical enough that i think i&#039;m qualified to make choices independent of the legislatures, but some people aren&#039;t. i&#039;d like to feel from people who fit under both categories (thank you for being a participant, cin)....<br /><br />o and while i think immortal technique has yet to show himself to be a revolutionary, you don&#039;t need to march or carry a gun to fit the bill. you just gotta have the figurative balls and the belief, plus a drive to do anything at all (i&#039;m mostly lacking the last one).</div>
						</td>
					</tr>
